WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:10.000
Lone Ranger

00:10.000 --> 00:24.000
A fiery horse with a speed of light, a cloud of dust, and a hearty hiyo silver.

00:24.000 --> 00:26.000
The Lone Ranger

00:26.000 --> 00:36.000
This is a story of one of the most mysterious characters to appear in the early days of the West.

00:36.000 --> 00:45.000
He was a fabulous individual, a man whose presence brought fear to the lawless and hope to those who wanted to make this frontier land their home.

00:45.000 --> 00:48.000
He was known as the Lone Ranger.

00:48.000 --> 01:01.000
Before his coming, this new land of the West was a wild, unruly territory into which brave American pioneers moved in covered wagons, on horseback, and afoot.

01:01.000 --> 01:07.000
Theirs was a rugged existence, for they not only had to settle and build, they had to fight.

01:07.000 --> 01:14.000
Here beyond the reach of law and order, might was right. The best shot was the best man.

01:14.000 --> 01:22.000
Born of necessity in this chaotic period of Westward expansion, an organization was developed to combat the evil forces of the times.

01:22.000 --> 01:26.000
An organization called the Texas Rangers.

01:26.000 --> 01:33.000
Ever on the job of maintaining law and order, six Texas Rangers ride alertly across a western landscape.

01:33.000 --> 01:36.000
All are courageous, straight-shooting men.

01:36.000 --> 01:47.000
At their head is a stranger, a half-breed scout, who one day earlier had appeared at a ranger in Kaplan, suffering from a severe wound.

01:47.000 --> 01:50.000
There you are, Collins. Nothing to worry about now.

01:50.000 --> 01:55.000
Thanks, Captain. I should worry about a wound. I'm lucky to be alive.

01:55.000 --> 02:00.000
You're right. Very few people are after those outlaws have struck.

02:00.000 --> 02:02.000
So they killed everybody at the trading post?

02:02.000 --> 02:08.000
Everyone. Mr. Simpson, his wife, the young Indian helper, even old Pete McHenry.

02:08.000 --> 02:10.000
That's too bad.

02:10.000 --> 02:13.000
How about the outlaws? Any of them got shot?

02:13.000 --> 02:17.000
Four of them were in pretty bad shape. They had to be tied to their horses to ride away.

02:17.000 --> 02:19.000
I got two of them myself.

02:19.000 --> 02:21.000
Too bad you couldn't hit more of them.

02:21.000 --> 02:23.000
Which way did they go?

02:23.000 --> 02:25.000
They rode up north, into the Badlands.

02:25.000 --> 02:27.000
That's pretty rough country.

02:27.000 --> 02:31.000
But with wounded men in tow, they can't travel very fast.

02:31.000 --> 02:33.000
We got a chance to catch them.

02:33.000 --> 02:37.000
They have a five-hour start. Even if you overtook them, they outnumber you.

02:37.000 --> 02:40.000
Mister, the rangers are used to long odds.

02:40.000 --> 02:44.000
How about you? You could use your help in locating their trail if you feel strong enough to ride.

02:44.000 --> 02:49.000
I don't need two arms to guide a horse. Besides, I've escort we even up two.

02:49.000 --> 02:51.000
I'll go alone.

02:51.000 --> 02:52.000
Good man.

02:52.000 --> 02:56.000
Boys, pack five days rations. We've got ten minutes to be in the saddle.

02:56.000 --> 02:58.000
Right. Bill, send Captain Reed to me.

02:58.000 --> 02:59.000
He's leading your detachment.

02:59.000 --> 03:01.000
Yes, sir.

03:01.000 --> 03:09.000
So came the half-breed Collins, who rides with a detachment of rangers after the most ruthless outlaw band in the West.

03:09.000 --> 03:12.000
The notorious Cavendish Gang.

03:18.000 --> 03:19.000
Is that trail, Reed?

03:19.000 --> 03:21.000
Yeah.

03:21.000 --> 03:24.000
It indicates they're riding slowly as we figured.

03:24.000 --> 03:26.000
Yes, it does.

03:26.000 --> 03:29.000
Let's go, boys.

03:56.000 --> 04:01.000
We've gained on them. These tracks can't be more than three hours old.

04:01.000 --> 04:03.000
They're heading up into that canyon.

04:03.000 --> 04:06.000
Yeah, it looks that way.

04:06.000 --> 04:08.000
Have you been in this vicinity before?

04:08.000 --> 04:10.000
Couple of times.

04:10.000 --> 04:14.000
I think it might be a good idea for us to check that canyon from the rim first.

04:14.000 --> 04:16.000
I'll go ahead and scout the place.

04:16.000 --> 04:19.000
One man would be a lot less noticeable than all of us going.

04:19.000 --> 04:22.000
All right. We'll wait here and give the horses a rest.

04:22.000 --> 04:26.000
Dismount, men.

04:52.000 --> 04:55.000
Hold it, mister. Reach.

04:55.000 --> 04:57.000
Hello, Whitey.

04:57.000 --> 04:59.000
Ah, it's you.

04:59.000 --> 05:01.000
The new man, huh?

05:01.000 --> 05:03.000
Yeah, it's me.

05:03.000 --> 05:04.000
How's the plan going?

05:04.000 --> 05:07.000
All right. The ranger's at the back of the canyon entrance.

05:07.000 --> 05:09.000
You think they'll keep on coming?

05:09.000 --> 05:10.000
Sure.

05:10.000 --> 05:12.000
I'll be back in a minute.

05:12.000 --> 05:14.000
All right.

05:14.000 --> 05:16.000
I'll be back in a minute.

05:16.000 --> 05:17.000
All right.

05:17.000 --> 05:19.000
I'll be back in a minute.

05:19.000 --> 05:22.000
How's the plan going?

05:22.000 --> 05:23.000
All right.

05:23.000 --> 05:26.000
The ranger's at the back of the canyon entrance.

05:26.000 --> 05:28.000
You think they'll keep on coming?

05:28.000 --> 05:30.000
Sure. Why not?

05:30.000 --> 05:32.000
They're pretty smart umbers, you know.

05:32.000 --> 05:33.000
Yeah.

05:33.000 --> 05:35.000
We've tried to get them before. Didn't pan out.

05:35.000 --> 05:38.000
I wasn't with you then. Where's Cavendish?

05:38.000 --> 05:41.000
He's up there, always. Leave your horse here.

05:41.000 --> 05:49.000
I knew they'd fall for my little trap, Collins.

05:49.000 --> 05:53.000
That bullet I put in your arm really convinced them.

05:53.000 --> 05:54.000
Yeah, Cavendish.

05:54.000 --> 05:55.000
It sure did.

05:55.000 --> 06:01.000
When I get through around here, it's going to be a long time before any ranger outfit patrols in this section of the country.

06:01.000 --> 06:03.000
We're going to make this something they won't forget.

06:03.000 --> 06:05.000
Is the ambush all set?

06:05.000 --> 06:26.000
Take a gander over there.

06:26.000 --> 06:31.000
You know, my men could slaughter a whole army if I'm up here.

06:31.000 --> 06:35.000
It looks good to me.

06:35.000 --> 06:39.000
Now we're going to hold our fire until the lawmen ride in directly below us.

06:39.000 --> 06:43.000
Jerry, you tell the boys I'll shoot first. That'll be the signal to open up on the rangers.

06:43.000 --> 06:44.000
Right.

06:44.000 --> 06:48.000
And Jerry, when a man goes down, I want him there to be kept pouring into him until he looks like a shield.

06:48.000 --> 06:49.000
You got that straight?

06:49.000 --> 06:50.000
You bet.

06:50.000 --> 06:51.000
Pull up, Leckie.

06:51.000 --> 06:52.000
All right.

06:52.000 --> 06:54.000
Collins, you're doing a good job for a new man.

06:54.000 --> 06:55.000
Thanks, Chief.

06:55.000 --> 06:57.000
Now get back there and bring on the Texas rangers.

06:57.000 --> 06:58.000
Yeah.

06:58.000 --> 07:14.000
Sure.

07:14.000 --> 07:15.000
See anything?

07:15.000 --> 07:19.000
Yes, but not on the rim.

07:19.000 --> 07:24.000
The Cavendish officer is just leaving the far side of the canyon, about four miles off.

07:24.000 --> 07:25.000
Are you sure?

07:25.000 --> 07:27.000
I saw their dust.

07:27.000 --> 07:28.000
Good.

07:28.000 --> 07:57.000
Here we go, ma'am.

07:57.000 --> 08:26.000
Good.

08:26.000 --> 08:28.000
Must have taken a wrong turn.

08:28.000 --> 08:29.000
Look up there.

08:29.000 --> 08:30.000
It's a head wall.

08:30.000 --> 08:33.000
Don't pass through here.

08:33.000 --> 08:35.000
Say, where's Collins?

08:35.000 --> 08:36.000
He was right behind us a while ago.

08:36.000 --> 08:37.000
I saw him.

08:37.000 --> 08:38.000
Look.

08:38.000 --> 08:39.000
It's an ambush, men.

08:39.000 --> 08:42.000
Spread out and find cover quick.

08:42.000 --> 09:09.000
Yes, sir.

09:09.000 --> 09:22.000
Got a chance, that half-breed crater, Collins.

09:22.000 --> 09:50.000
Well.

09:50.000 --> 09:52.000
Hey, man, hold it.

09:52.000 --> 10:01.000
Hold it.

10:01.000 --> 10:03.000
Hey, Collins.

10:03.000 --> 10:05.000
Yes, Chief?

10:05.000 --> 10:07.000
Check those men down there.

10:07.000 --> 10:32.000
I saw them.

10:32.000 --> 10:57.000
I saw them.

10:57.000 --> 11:04.000
You got them all?

11:04.000 --> 11:05.000
Good.

11:05.000 --> 11:07.000
Go on back to your horse.

11:07.000 --> 11:26.000
We'll wait for you up here.

11:26.000 --> 11:28.000
I thought he was one of us, Chief.

11:28.000 --> 11:29.000
He was.

11:29.000 --> 11:33.000
Any man who would betray those Rangers might be just as quick to turn on us, I figured.

11:33.000 --> 11:35.000
And no one does that.

11:35.000 --> 11:39.000
It's gonna be a nice little reminder for anybody who tries to stop me from now on.

11:39.000 --> 11:40.000
Yeah.

11:40.000 --> 11:41.000
Now let's get out of here.

11:41.000 --> 12:01.000
I've got work to do in Colby.

12:01.000 --> 12:09.000
Many hours after the Cavendish gang has left the ambush scene, a lone figure moves painfully over the rock-strewn ground.

12:09.000 --> 12:12.000
The whole spring, gurgling in a shady cave, is his destination.

12:12.000 --> 12:16.000
He alone of the six Texas Rangers still lives.

12:16.000 --> 12:20.000
His one aim is to reach water and shade.

12:20.000 --> 12:25.000
Desperately, he fights off the weakness from loss of blood, which threatens to halt his progress.

12:25.000 --> 12:27.000
It is deathly hot.

12:27.000 --> 12:34.000
The sun beats down unmercifully.

12:34.000 --> 12:38.000
Ahead is that cooling, life-giving water.

12:38.000 --> 12:42.000
His strength can only last.

12:42.000 --> 12:52.000
Foot by foot, sheer torture, he pulls himself along.

12:52.000 --> 13:03.000
There, out of the hot glare of the sun, only a short distance farther.

13:03.000 --> 13:05.000
There's the spring.

13:05.000 --> 13:11.000
Now his bandana.

13:11.000 --> 13:13.000
There.

13:13.000 --> 13:18.000
Cold water never felt better, never seen sweeter than now.

13:18.000 --> 13:25.000
A hundred yards away, Colin stirs, unaware of the existence of the spring and the Ranger who lies beside him.

13:25.000 --> 13:31.000
He too, miraculously, has escaped a mortal wound from a Cavendish bullet.

13:31.000 --> 13:37.000
He too, like the Ranger, knows that death lurks in the hot rays of the sun.

13:37.000 --> 13:45.000
He must seek shelter somewhere, quickly, within the confines of the canyon.

13:45.000 --> 13:50.000
Weekly, he stumbles off to find shade to nurse his wounds.

13:50.000 --> 13:57.000
Then, as if timed by fate, a third figure, an Indian, enters the hot canyon.

13:57.000 --> 14:02.000
Surprised, he sees the results of the Cavendish gang's handiwork.

14:02.000 --> 14:08.000
Texas Rangers sprawled in attitudes of violent death.

14:08.000 --> 14:13.000
These were men to whom heroic deeds were a part of each day's work.

14:13.000 --> 14:18.000
And now their work was done.

14:18.000 --> 14:22.000
Then, by chance, the native spies the figure lying beside the distant pool.

14:22.000 --> 14:35.000
He moves forward.

14:35.000 --> 14:47.000
I still may not hurt you.

14:47.000 --> 14:50.000
Why, you, you Kimo Sabe.

14:50.000 --> 14:55.000
Kimo Sabe? That sounds familiar.

14:55.000 --> 14:59.000
That's right, Kimo Sabe. You trusty scout.

14:59.000 --> 15:01.000
Trusty scout?

15:01.000 --> 15:06.000
Yes, Ranger. Long time back, when we both young.

15:06.000 --> 15:09.000
Me remember time Indian camp burned.

15:09.000 --> 15:12.000
Renegade Indians raid settlement when men of tribe away.

15:12.000 --> 15:16.000
Kill my mother, my sisters. They leave me for dead.

15:16.000 --> 15:21.000
You found me. Nurse me back to health. Save me from dying.

15:21.000 --> 15:24.000
When me well, you give me horse to go find my father.

15:24.000 --> 15:27.000
Me take horse only when you accept gift.

15:27.000 --> 15:29.000
My ring, it make good luck.

15:29.000 --> 15:32.000
Me call you Kimo Sabe.

15:32.000 --> 15:37.000
It mean trusty scout. Me never forget you.

15:37.000 --> 15:38.000
You remember now?

15:38.000 --> 15:43.000
Yes. You, you're Tonto.

15:43.000 --> 15:52.000
That's right. Me Tonto. Now me take care of you.

15:52.000 --> 16:00.000
Immediately the Indian, Tonto, unsaddles his horse and sets up a camp at the entrance of the cave near the pool and the injured man.

16:00.000 --> 16:09.000
The ranger's wounds are cleansed and expertly dressed by the Indian, who calls upon all the lore he knows to ease his white friend's suffering.

16:09.000 --> 16:15.000
Several days of patient vigil, tender care and careful feeding bring results.

16:15.000 --> 16:20.000
The ranger gradually regains his strength. He is going to live.

16:20.000 --> 16:27.000
Tonto, I guess I drifted off again. How long have I been here now?

16:27.000 --> 16:29.000
Three days. How do you feel?

16:29.000 --> 16:35.000
Still a little weak. The other rangers, Tonto, all dead.

16:35.000 --> 16:36.000
Hmm.

16:36.000 --> 16:40.000
One of them, Captain Reed, was my brother.

16:40.000 --> 16:44.000
Too bad. Rangers all fine men.

16:44.000 --> 16:53.000
We didn't have a chance, Tonto. It was a perfect ambush and double cross. How did you happen to find me?

16:53.000 --> 17:00.000
Me hunt here in Canyon often. Ride in here on scout over yonder. Find rangers and dead horse.

17:00.000 --> 17:01.000
I see.

17:01.000 --> 17:11.000
While you sleep, me bury other rangers over there. Make grave for men. Bring belongings here.

17:11.000 --> 17:12.000
That was good of you, Tonto.

17:12.000 --> 17:14.000
Them brave men.

17:14.000 --> 17:22.000
Yes, they were brave. And they won't be forgotten. I've spent a lot of time thinking.

17:22.000 --> 17:27.000
For every one of those men, I'm going to bring a hundred lawbreakers to justice.

17:27.000 --> 17:35.000
I'll make that Cavendish gang and every criminal that I can find for that matter regret the day those rangers were killed.

17:35.000 --> 17:45.000
Tonto, from this moment on, I'm going to devote my life to establishing law and order in this new frontier to make the West a decent place to live.

17:45.000 --> 17:47.000
That good.

17:47.000 --> 17:54.000
But when Cavendish gang know you escape ambush, you'll mark men. They hunt you down, many against one.

17:54.000 --> 18:01.000
No one is going to know I'm alive. I'm supposed to be dead and I'm going to stay that way.

18:01.000 --> 18:06.000
I'll hide my identity somehow. I'll wear a disguise of some sort.

18:06.000 --> 18:08.000
You mean like mask?

18:08.000 --> 18:14.000
That's it, Tonto. From now on, I'll wear a mask.

18:14.000 --> 18:19.000
Let's see. There ought to be some material here I can use.

18:19.000 --> 18:25.000
Here. This. My brother's vest.

18:25.000 --> 18:28.000
It belonged to one of the bravest of them all.

18:28.000 --> 18:31.000
Me help you, Masardi. You rest now.

18:31.000 --> 18:34.000
All right, Tonto. Thanks.

18:41.000 --> 18:44.000
There, face means all healed now.

18:44.000 --> 18:49.000
Let me have the mask.

18:50.000 --> 18:56.000
There. It fits perfectly. Good job, Tonto.

18:56.000 --> 19:01.000
Here hat. Me washing stream. Guy and son make whiter.

19:01.000 --> 19:04.000
Thanks, Tonto.

19:04.000 --> 19:07.000
Here gun to kill bad men.

19:07.000 --> 19:09.000
I'm not going to do any killing.

19:09.000 --> 19:11.000
You will not defend yourself?

19:11.000 --> 19:16.000
I'll shoot if I have to. But I'll shoot the wound, not to kill.

19:16.000 --> 19:19.000
A man must die. It's up to the law to decide that.

19:19.000 --> 19:21.000
Not the person behind the six shooter.

19:21.000 --> 19:23.000
That's right, Kimo Sabe.

19:23.000 --> 19:26.000
Well, there's just one more thing to be done.

19:26.000 --> 19:27.000
What's that?

19:27.000 --> 19:31.000
Dig another grave. Out there.

19:31.000 --> 19:33.000
Who that's for?

19:33.000 --> 19:45.000
That'll be my grave.

19:45.000 --> 19:48.000
There. Just like others.

19:48.000 --> 19:50.000
That's good.

19:50.000 --> 19:53.000
Only you, Tonto, know I'm alive.

19:53.000 --> 19:55.000
To the world I'll be buried here.

19:55.000 --> 19:58.000
Beside my brother and my friends.

19:58.000 --> 19:59.000
Forever.

19:59.000 --> 20:02.000
You all alone now. Last man.

20:02.000 --> 20:05.000
You are a lone ranger.

20:05.000 --> 20:11.000
Yes, Tonto. I am a lone ranger.

20:11.000 --> 20:14.000
Kimo Sabe, me help you fight outlaw.

20:14.000 --> 20:18.000
But, Tonto, don't you have a family? Anyone?

20:18.000 --> 20:23.000
No. Me lone like you. Me want law here too. For all.

20:23.000 --> 20:27.000
All right, Tonto. You'll be a lot of help.

20:27.000 --> 20:29.000
We'll ride together.

20:29.000 --> 20:32.000
Me glad, Kimo Sabe. Me fight good for you.

20:32.000 --> 20:38.000
Take cover, Tonto.

20:38.000 --> 20:40.000
That's Collins. A man that led us into ambush.

20:40.000 --> 20:41.000
Why him shoot?

20:41.000 --> 20:44.000
Undoubtedly he's after your horse.

20:44.000 --> 20:46.000
Collins must have been deserted by the outlaws.

20:46.000 --> 20:50.000
He's probably been making his way to that vantage point for some time now.

20:50.000 --> 20:52.000
Why him go there, Kimo Sabe?

20:52.000 --> 20:55.000
He knows the only way to get scouted is to kill us.

20:55.000 --> 20:57.000
He is well protected up there.

20:57.000 --> 20:58.000
What we do now?

20:58.000 --> 21:03.000
I'm going to keep him back under cover while you make a run for scout.

21:03.000 --> 21:06.000
Take scout into the cave. Collins won't be able to see you from there.

21:06.000 --> 21:08.000
Me do.

21:08.000 --> 21:10.000
I'll follow Tonto as soon as you're safe.

21:10.000 --> 21:38.000
All right. Run.

21:38.000 --> 21:39.000
Did you hit him?

21:39.000 --> 21:41.000
No, Tonto. I didn't try.

21:41.000 --> 21:42.000
We're going to capture him alive.

21:42.000 --> 21:45.000
That hard? Him in a good spot?

21:45.000 --> 21:48.000
Yes, but I have an idea how we can do it.

21:48.000 --> 21:51.000
You start up the cliff on that side. First tie up scout.

21:51.000 --> 22:20.000
I'll try to draw his fire from the other side of the rocks.

22:20.000 --> 22:25.000
I'll try to draw his fire from the other side of the rocks.

22:25.000 --> 22:50.000
I'll try to draw his fire from the other side of the rocks.

22:50.000 --> 23:10.000
I'll try to draw his fire from the other side of the rocks.

23:10.000 --> 23:30.000
I'll try to draw his fire from the other side of the rocks.

23:30.000 --> 23:53.000
I'll try to draw his fire from the other side of the rocks.

23:53.000 --> 24:13.000
I'll try to draw his fire from the other side of the rocks.

24:13.000 --> 24:38.000
I'll try to draw his fire from the other side of the rocks.

24:38.000 --> 25:03.000
I'll try to draw his fire from the other side of the rocks.

25:03.000 --> 25:31.000
I'll try to draw his fire from the other side of the rocks.

25:31.000 --> 25:39.000
Later at the entrance to this valley, the Lone Ranger and Tonto hear sounds of a furious battle.

25:39.000 --> 25:45.000
Beyond the rocks in a small glen, they catch a glimpse of a huge buffalo about to gore the life out of a fallen horse.

25:45.000 --> 25:52.000
Quickly the Lone Ranger reaches for his gun.

25:52.000 --> 26:14.000
You hit him, Kibosaki. I hope it was soon enough, Tonto.

26:14.000 --> 26:22.000
The buffalo is dying. The horse looks bad. Can you shoot him? No, Tonto. I'm going to try to pull him through.

26:22.000 --> 26:27.000
Well, old fellow, you're in bad shape. A lot of bumps and bruises.

26:27.000 --> 26:32.000
Tonto, get some rags from the supplies and bring the canteen. Me do.

26:32.000 --> 26:41.000
So in an instant, the Lone Ranger has made a momentous decision, for he has recognized the sterling qualities of the animal he has saved.

26:41.000 --> 26:46.000
For some time, the masked man in the Indian camp decides a wounded horse.

26:46.000 --> 26:50.000
Tending his battle hurts and caring for him as best they know how.

26:50.000 --> 26:55.000
Now human hands have done all they can to help nature in healing the animal's wounds.

26:55.000 --> 27:02.000
Can the wild stallion rise? Gently the masked man coaxes the horse.

27:02.000 --> 27:09.000
Easy, easy, big fellow. Come on, on your feet. Come on, boy. Come on, boy.

27:09.000 --> 27:13.000
Easy, big fellow. Easy.

27:26.000 --> 27:30.000
He's got bridle and lariat. Him run away. No, wait, Tonto.

27:30.000 --> 27:43.000
I'd like that horse more than anything in the world. But if he wants to go, he should be free.

27:49.000 --> 27:56.000
Him a beauty. Like mountain with snow. Silver white.

27:56.000 --> 28:13.000
Silver. That would be a name for him. Here, Silver. Come back, big fellow. Come on, Silver. Come back, big fellow. Come on, Silver.

28:13.000 --> 28:24.000
Me get bridle now. Him come back. No, he wouldn't take a bit. I'll use a hackamore for the time being.

28:31.000 --> 28:34.000
Won't be as strange to his headers as bridle.

28:34.000 --> 28:44.000
There. There, Silver. You only knew how we need you.

28:44.000 --> 28:52.000
Ooh. Oh, boy. Ooh. Ooh. Ooh, big fellow. Ready?

28:52.000 --> 29:01.000
Going to do a lot of riding together. Going to be paled like we Silver.

29:01.000 --> 29:08.000
A few days later, after the horse has recovered his strength, the lone ranger tightens the saddle on the back of the white stallion.

29:08.000 --> 29:14.000
Quickly, the masked man mounts. And for the first time in his life, Silver bears weight upon his back.

29:14.000 --> 29:20.000
Soon, under expert and gentle guidance, the horse quiets and responds quickly to the lone ranger's lead.

29:25.000 --> 29:31.000
Later, the hackamore is exchanged for a bridle in bit. Here is no conflict between animal and master.

29:31.000 --> 29:37.000
Here instead is a partnership between horse and rider. The horse and rider are a couple.

29:37.000 --> 29:47.000
Here is no conflict between animal and master. Here instead is a partnership between horse and rider. The lone ranger and Silver accept each other as equals.

29:55.000 --> 29:57.000
These are beauty titles.

29:57.000 --> 30:05.000
A dream horse if I ever rode one. Him and Scout good friends. Yes. They'll do a lot of riding side by side.

30:05.000 --> 30:13.000
Ah. Me got everything packed. Here's saddlebag. Here's blanket.

30:13.000 --> 30:21.000
We ride after Cavendish Gang now? Yes, Tonno. They were headed toward Colby.

30:21.000 --> 30:27.000
Then through there's an old timer there I want to see by the name of Jim Blaine. All right, let's go.

30:52.000 --> 30:58.000
There it is, Tonno. Jim Blaine's place. Look like him got company.

30:58.000 --> 31:02.000
We'll wait until the caller leaves before we drop in.

31:02.000 --> 31:08.000
It was nice seeing you, Judge. We'll see you next week, Jim.

31:08.000 --> 31:28.000
Look, the side of the house. Come on.

31:31.000 --> 31:34.000
Got him, Chief. Good.

31:34.000 --> 31:42.000
But he winged me. Is he out? He's cold. He won't be seeing light for hours. Trade guns.

31:42.000 --> 31:45.000
Say, you're bleeding bad. Yeah, must have hit an artery.

31:45.000 --> 31:53.000
You get down the road pronto and spread the news to the chute and then get me a doc. I'll meet you at the glen. All right.

31:53.000 --> 32:18.000
Someone's coming. We got to clear out of here fast.

32:18.000 --> 32:24.000
Him that young, the Chimisabi. How's Jim? He's had a hard knock.

32:24.000 --> 32:29.000
Me thought maybe murders killed Jim, too. They could have, Tonno.

32:29.000 --> 32:34.000
For some reason, they wanted Jim to be found here alive.

32:34.000 --> 32:39.000
He's coming, too. Help me carry him to the porch.

32:39.000 --> 32:44.000
So that's why I'm after Cavendish, Jim. The reason for the mask.

32:44.000 --> 32:49.000
My real identity is to be kept a secret, known only to the three of us.

32:49.000 --> 32:54.000
Well, I'll be hanged. You sure got a job cut out for yourself, Reed.

32:54.000 --> 32:58.000
I mean, Lone Ranger. I'll help him any way I can.

32:58.000 --> 33:02.000
I was hoping you'd join us, Jim, and live up on our old silver mine.

33:02.000 --> 33:07.000
Oh, you're mighty kind to an old ex-ranger who used to nurse me, Jevoy.

33:07.000 --> 33:12.000
Sure would like to. Never did like living here trying to farm, only...

33:12.000 --> 33:17.000
Only what? Well, I can't run out now with the owner of the bank.

33:17.000 --> 33:22.000
Poor old Judge Knockfly and Dead back there. Folks might think that I did it.

33:22.000 --> 33:26.000
Why, they might start blaming me for all the rest of the killings that's going on in town.

33:26.000 --> 33:30.000
More murders, Jim? Yes, lots of them.

33:30.000 --> 33:34.000
First, it was Glenn Peters. He owns a newspaper.

33:34.000 --> 33:39.000
Then Biff Anderson, postmaster and telegraph man combined.

33:39.000 --> 33:44.000
Then Max Strauss, the hotel man. And a couple others, too.

33:44.000 --> 33:48.000
Why, it seems everybody important in Colby's getting shot.

33:48.000 --> 33:51.000
The Sheriff's Club going crazy.

33:51.000 --> 33:54.000
Did you recognize the man who killed the judge?

33:54.000 --> 33:59.000
Couldn't mistake him. It was Butch Cavendish. Now who'd you say you're after?

33:59.000 --> 34:05.000
Cavendish? He must be doing these killings in Colby for a very special reason.

34:05.000 --> 34:11.000
Yeah, but why? Most likely he wanted the murders to look like a local affair.

34:11.000 --> 34:17.000
I'm certain Jim will upset his plans if you're not around to accept the blame for the judge's death.

34:17.000 --> 34:21.000
That old silver mine of ours will be a perfect hideout for you.

34:21.000 --> 34:27.000
Yes, it would. My Cranky, I guess you're right.

34:27.000 --> 34:33.000
It would be best if I became scarce around here for a bit. I'll go along with you.

34:33.000 --> 34:37.000
That's fine. How does knee tunnel? Leg better now.

34:37.000 --> 34:41.000
Good. Help me put Jim on Silver's back.

34:44.000 --> 34:49.000
A few hours later, after a tedious ride over winding trails and unknown paths,

34:49.000 --> 34:54.000
the Lone Ranger, Jim and Tonto, approach the site of the secret silver mine.

34:54.000 --> 34:58.000
This is it, Tonto. Where am I?

34:58.000 --> 35:02.000
Don't say it, do your engine. Fooled you just like everybody else.

35:02.000 --> 35:05.000
Come on, I'll show you.

35:13.000 --> 35:15.000
Here we are, Tonto.

35:15.000 --> 35:19.000
Must have been ten years ago that my brother, old Jim,

35:19.000 --> 35:24.000
here and I discovered the mine and built this shed.

35:24.000 --> 35:28.000
Nine years ago this was still an OC mine.

35:28.000 --> 35:31.000
Over here, Tonto.

35:31.000 --> 35:34.000
It's under the bunk.

35:34.000 --> 35:36.000
There.

35:36.000 --> 35:40.000
Yep, one of the richest veins of silver ever found in these here parts.

35:40.000 --> 35:44.000
I never thought I'd share about digging up the ore.

35:44.000 --> 35:47.000
That is, until now.

35:47.000 --> 35:49.000
Water there, too.

35:49.000 --> 35:53.000
That's the spring we were cleaning up when we found the vein.

35:53.000 --> 35:57.000
We drained the water to get to the silver ore.

35:57.000 --> 36:03.000
Jim, you and I can fix up this place while Tanner goes into town for the sheriff.

36:03.000 --> 36:05.000
His name is Taylor, isn't it?

36:05.000 --> 36:08.000
Yes, old, two guns, still on the job.

36:08.000 --> 36:10.000
All right, Chimo-Savvy.

36:10.000 --> 36:13.000
When you find the sheriff, tell him you know where Jim Blaine is.

36:13.000 --> 36:15.000
He'll come with you.

36:15.000 --> 36:18.000
That's good. Me go now.

36:20.000 --> 36:22.000
The shack seems to be in pretty good shape.

36:22.000 --> 36:24.000
I'll be as snug as a bug.

36:24.000 --> 36:28.000
All I want out of this place is enough silver for some immediate needs.

36:28.000 --> 36:31.000
Other than that, all you can dig is yours.

36:31.000 --> 36:33.000
Oh, now I couldn't.

36:33.000 --> 36:35.000
You're entitled to it, Jim.

36:35.000 --> 36:37.000
You'll have to do all the work.

36:37.000 --> 36:41.000
You know, I could refine a little ore right here.

36:41.000 --> 36:45.000
That's just what I want you to do, Jim.

36:45.000 --> 36:48.000
Then in the future, whenever Tano and I come back here,

36:48.000 --> 36:51.000
we can get the pure metal for money and bullets.

36:51.000 --> 36:52.000
Bullets?

36:52.000 --> 36:53.000
Yes.

36:53.000 --> 36:56.000
I want some of the ore cast into silver bullets.

36:56.000 --> 37:00.000
Some ration, what's the matter with lead bullets? They'll kill Jim as well as you.

37:00.000 --> 37:03.000
You forget I told you I vowed never to shoot to kill.

37:03.000 --> 37:07.000
Silver bullets will serve as sort of a symbol.

37:07.000 --> 37:09.000
Tano suggested the idea.

37:09.000 --> 37:12.000
Symbol? Of what?

37:12.000 --> 37:15.000
A symbol which means justice by law.

37:15.000 --> 37:20.000
I wanted to become known to all who see the silver bullets.

37:20.000 --> 37:23.000
But I live and fight only to see the eventual defeat

37:23.000 --> 37:27.000
and the proper punishment by law of every criminal in the West.

37:29.000 --> 37:33.000
By criminy, I think you've got something there.

37:33.000 --> 37:37.000
And I'll mold you all the silver bullets you want, Ranger.

37:37.000 --> 37:41.000
But from what I know about this here country,

37:41.000 --> 37:45.000
there'll be an awful lot of silver shot up before you're through.

37:45.000 --> 37:47.000
I hope not, Jim.

37:47.000 --> 37:49.000
We have a lot of metal here for that purpose.

37:49.000 --> 37:51.000
If we need it.

37:58.000 --> 38:01.000
Now, those dirty killers. I wish I knew where to look for them first.

38:01.000 --> 38:05.000
Now, Pete, you look up at Old Creek Station for Jim.

38:05.000 --> 38:10.000
Clem, go get Creel and fetch the Doc's body and bring it back into town.

38:10.000 --> 38:13.000
This is a fine time for Doc Drummond to be missing.

38:13.000 --> 38:15.000
Jim Blaine disappeared.

38:15.000 --> 38:18.000
I never suspected he'd ever have anything to do with these killers.

38:18.000 --> 38:22.000
Sharon, go away and detect if he's busy.

38:22.000 --> 38:26.000
Now, Bill Keith says that Jim's course is still at his farm.

38:26.000 --> 38:29.000
Oh, he must have gone off on foot. Now, he can't be very far.

38:29.000 --> 38:31.000
Me know where Jim Blaine is.

38:31.000 --> 38:34.000
Look, Indian, will you go away, please?

38:34.000 --> 38:37.000
Can't you see I'm busy with these men?

38:37.000 --> 38:39.000
What'd you say?

38:39.000 --> 38:42.000
Me know where Jim Blaine is. Him want to see you.

38:42.000 --> 38:45.000
Well, you know where he is. Why didn't you say so? Where is he?

38:45.000 --> 38:47.000
Me not tell.

38:47.000 --> 38:49.000
What do you mean by that? You just said you knew.

38:49.000 --> 38:51.000
You come. Me take you to Jim.

38:51.000 --> 38:54.000
Now, wait a minute. How do I know that you know where Jim is?

38:54.000 --> 38:57.000
You don't know, but you follow me.

38:57.000 --> 39:00.000
Jim wants to talk to you about Judge Knox's killing.

39:00.000 --> 39:02.000
I bet he does, all right.

39:02.000 --> 39:06.000
I want to talk to him, too. Hank, you and I are a sad lot. We're gonna follow this, Indian.

39:06.000 --> 39:08.000
No one comes. Only you.

39:08.000 --> 39:12.000
Now, look, you don't think I'd be silly enough to go with you alone, do you?

39:12.000 --> 39:17.000
Me think so. You brave lawman. Want to solve mysteries.

39:17.000 --> 39:20.000
If you're right. Well, maybe you're right, then.

39:20.000 --> 39:22.000
And how long a ride is it?

39:22.000 --> 39:27.000
Not far for a tough sheriff. Maybe two hours away.

39:27.000 --> 39:29.000
All right, Indian. You lead the way.

39:29.000 --> 39:32.000
Don't forget. Any funny business?

39:32.000 --> 39:34.000
Hank, keep an eye on things while I come back.

39:34.000 --> 39:36.000
All right, see you, then.

39:46.000 --> 39:47.000
Good evening, stranger.

39:47.000 --> 39:49.000
What's up, my good sheriff?

39:49.000 --> 39:50.000
I can explain.

39:50.000 --> 39:51.000
How are these two guys?

39:51.000 --> 39:54.000
Jim, what the hell is going on here?

39:54.000 --> 39:55.000
Who are these people?

39:55.000 --> 39:56.000
Who's this masked man?

39:56.000 --> 39:58.000
What about Judge Knox's shooting?

39:58.000 --> 40:02.000
Somebody's got a heap of explaining to do.

40:02.000 --> 40:06.000
And after the killers knocked me out and changed guns with me,

40:06.000 --> 40:09.000
my friend and his engine pal brought me here.

40:12.000 --> 40:14.000
Very interesting story, Jim.

40:14.000 --> 40:18.000
What's more interesting, maybe, is that you've convinced me the masked man here

40:18.000 --> 40:20.000
is fighting on the right side of the law.

40:20.000 --> 40:22.000
I'm glad you're convinced, sheriff.

40:22.000 --> 40:25.000
So Butch Cavendish is the judge of murder, huh?

40:25.000 --> 40:30.000
Yep, and right now he's somewhere in this here vicinity wounded with my bullet in it.

40:30.000 --> 40:32.000
Wounded, huh?

40:32.000 --> 40:35.000
Say, maybe that explains something.

40:35.000 --> 40:38.000
Well, Doc Drummond's the only doc in these parts, and he's missing.

40:38.000 --> 40:40.000
Ten to one, he's been fetched to fix up Cavendish.

40:40.000 --> 40:43.000
Yeah, the problem now is where is Cavendish hiding out?

40:43.000 --> 40:46.000
He left a trail leading away from Jim's farm.

40:46.000 --> 40:49.000
I think I might be able to follow it, sheriff.

40:49.000 --> 40:50.000
Good. I'll get some men from town.

40:50.000 --> 40:52.000
That'll take too much time.

40:52.000 --> 40:54.000
It would be quicker for you to come with me now.

40:54.000 --> 40:56.000
Tom can ride to town.

40:56.000 --> 40:58.000
Me do, then follow your trail.

40:58.000 --> 41:01.000
All right, engine, see my men Creel at the office.

41:01.000 --> 41:03.000
You'll get some men.

41:03.000 --> 41:05.000
Watch your head, Jim.

41:05.000 --> 41:08.000
Jim.

41:22.000 --> 41:25.000
Look here.

41:30.000 --> 41:32.000
Wagon track.

41:32.000 --> 41:37.000
Cavendish was followed by someone in a wagon, a horseman riding alongside of it.

41:37.000 --> 41:39.000
Those tracks were made by Doc Drummond's rig.

41:39.000 --> 41:42.000
He's got the only narrow rins around here.

41:42.000 --> 41:45.000
So far, so good. Let's go.

41:55.000 --> 41:57.000
Doc, take it easy.

41:57.000 --> 42:02.000
Well, I'm doing the best I can.

42:02.000 --> 42:05.000
That's it.

42:05.000 --> 42:08.000
All right, get on with the bandage, and I'm in a hurry.

42:08.000 --> 42:13.000
What's the matter? You scared of something like maybe the sheriff will find it?

42:13.000 --> 42:14.000
Why should I be?

42:14.000 --> 42:17.000
I'm getting rid of him just like I did everybody else of importance in Colby.

42:17.000 --> 42:21.000
You mean to say that you're responsible for all those murders that have happened in the last week?

42:21.000 --> 42:24.000
Sure. Might as well let you know.

42:24.000 --> 42:30.000
You see, Doc, all the men that have stepped into their vacated positions belong to my organization.

42:30.000 --> 42:32.000
It's taken a lot of planning, but we're just about set.

42:32.000 --> 42:34.000
I'm taking over Colby.

42:34.000 --> 42:36.000
Well, why are you telling me this?

42:36.000 --> 42:40.000
I'm going to be eating the saw bones in my organization, and I've just decided you're going to be him.

42:40.000 --> 42:42.000
Like blazes I am.

42:42.000 --> 42:45.000
I'd die before I'd help a pack of coyotes like him.

42:45.000 --> 42:46.000
What was that?

42:46.000 --> 42:48.000
A gun, Meg.

42:48.000 --> 42:50.000
Think he heard us.

42:50.000 --> 42:51.000
Somebody over there.

42:51.000 --> 42:52.000
Give me that gun.

42:52.000 --> 42:56.000
I'll cover the doc. You boys get over there and find out.

42:56.000 --> 43:22.000
Here they come. Take cover.

43:22.000 --> 43:24.000
Relax, Doc.

43:24.000 --> 43:27.000
Whatever's going on out there, my men will take care of it.

43:54.000 --> 43:59.000
Hold it.

43:59.000 --> 44:01.000
What's the blind spot, huh?

44:01.000 --> 44:02.000
What's these men, Sheriff?

44:02.000 --> 44:06.000
I'll go help the doc.

44:06.000 --> 44:08.000
Jerry!

44:08.000 --> 44:10.000
Pike!

44:10.000 --> 44:12.000
What's going on?

44:12.000 --> 44:14.000
Answer!

44:14.000 --> 44:17.000
You go take a look. I want to find out what's happening.

44:17.000 --> 44:26.000
But don't run. I've got this on your back.

44:26.000 --> 44:30.000
Cavendish, drop that gun.

44:30.000 --> 44:37.000
All right, Sheriff, bring in the others.

44:37.000 --> 44:38.000
You all right, Doc?

44:38.000 --> 44:39.000
Yeah, I'm all right.

44:39.000 --> 44:41.000
Who's the masked man?

44:41.000 --> 44:42.000
He's on the side of the law.

44:42.000 --> 44:44.000
Well, this crook was going to take over Colby.

44:44.000 --> 44:46.000
He admitted all those murders.

44:46.000 --> 44:48.000
We know, Doc. We overheard them.

44:48.000 --> 44:52.000
Sheriff, you can take Cavendish to town and Doc's buggy.

44:52.000 --> 44:55.000
These other three will tie up and put on their horses.

44:55.000 --> 44:57.000
Doc, you know where their mouths are?

44:57.000 --> 44:58.000
Yes, behind the rocks over yonder.

44:58.000 --> 45:00.000
Thanks.

45:00.000 --> 45:02.000
Here, Doc.

45:02.000 --> 45:05.000
Watch these out who's close, while I tie them up.

45:05.000 --> 45:09.000
Yeah, I hope they do make a move, especially this one.

45:09.000 --> 45:12.000
Remember this, boys. I'm not in jail yet.

45:12.000 --> 45:13.000
Shut up.

45:13.000 --> 45:16.000
Licking your mouth is making a move.

45:16.000 --> 45:23.000
Mind silver?

45:23.000 --> 45:29.000
Silver.

45:29.000 --> 45:36.000
What's wrong, Silver?

45:36.000 --> 45:38.000
Those are my men.

45:38.000 --> 45:43.000
I knew they were due here about this time.

45:43.000 --> 45:45.000
So you thought you had me, Sheriff.

45:45.000 --> 45:48.000
Well, you haven't.

45:48.000 --> 45:51.000
You, the sawbones, and that masked man over there behind those rocks

45:51.000 --> 45:53.000
can start counting the minutes you've got to live.

45:53.000 --> 45:56.000
Beginning right now.

45:56.000 --> 45:57.000
Silver.

45:57.000 --> 46:08.000
Silver.

46:08.000 --> 46:30.000
Silver.

46:30.000 --> 46:51.000
Look out, man. Something's happened to the boss.

46:51.000 --> 46:52.000
All right, boys. I got him.

46:52.000 --> 46:54.000
Get him high, Sheriff.

46:54.000 --> 46:56.000
Come on in, men.

46:56.000 --> 46:57.000
It's me, Cavendish.

46:57.000 --> 47:01.000
The shooting's over.

47:01.000 --> 47:03.000
Looks like we got here just in time, boss.

47:03.000 --> 47:04.000
You did. Tie these men up.

47:04.000 --> 47:06.000
What goes on?

47:06.000 --> 47:08.000
I got winged after a shot Judge Knox.

47:08.000 --> 47:11.000
The sawbones there was treating me when the sheriff and the masked man took things over.

47:11.000 --> 47:13.000
Is that the armory we seen ride off on the white horse?

47:13.000 --> 47:14.000
Yeah, which way did he go?

47:14.000 --> 47:16.000
Up the draw.

47:16.000 --> 47:19.000
It's all tied up, boss.

47:19.000 --> 47:21.000
Good. I'll watch him.

47:21.000 --> 47:24.000
You men get after that masked man before he gets too cold and spoils our plans.

47:24.000 --> 47:26.000
And bring him back dead or alive.

47:26.000 --> 47:31.000
You heard it, boys. Let's travel.

47:31.000 --> 47:37.000
Meanwhile, unaware of what has happened to the Lone Ranger since last he saw him, Tonto has arrived in the town of Colby.

47:37.000 --> 47:48.000
He's on his way to the sheriff's office to ask the sheriff's assistant, Alex Creel, for more men to aid the Lone Ranger in tracking down Butch Cavendish.

47:48.000 --> 47:52.000
The way you keep on cleaning that gun, Cory, you must be figuring on using it soon.

47:52.000 --> 47:54.000
Nothing like being prepared.

47:54.000 --> 47:59.000
Might have been going on around Colby lately and the shooting's only just begun.

47:59.000 --> 48:04.000
Creel says there's gonna be more of it tonight.

48:04.000 --> 48:06.000
Which one of you is sheriff's assistant?

48:06.000 --> 48:09.000
There's an offender risking comes looking for the sheriff.

48:09.000 --> 48:11.000
It's usually the other way around.

48:11.000 --> 48:12.000
What are you on, Indians?

48:12.000 --> 48:14.000
Me want to see Creel.

48:14.000 --> 48:18.000
At present, he's busy. I'm handling his business. What's on your mind?

48:18.000 --> 48:20.000
Sheriff Taylor sent me.

48:20.000 --> 48:22.000
Him on trail of Butch Cavendish.

48:22.000 --> 48:24.000
He asked for Creel to come with posse.

48:24.000 --> 48:26.000
Oh, he did, huh?

48:26.000 --> 48:30.000
You're lying, Indian. Everybody around here knows that Cavendish gang is nowhere around here.

48:30.000 --> 48:33.000
That's not so. I see Cavendish myself.

48:33.000 --> 48:36.000
You come. I'll show you trail.

48:36.000 --> 48:39.000
Will you listen to the Redskin ordering us around?

48:39.000 --> 48:44.000
I don't know what your game is, Indian, but if Sheriff Taylor really sent you, you'd have some kind of proof.

48:44.000 --> 48:47.000
You follow Tonto. You have plenty proof.

48:47.000 --> 48:50.000
Seems to me this Redskin's getting a little ornery, Cory. Should I throw him in jail?

48:50.000 --> 48:52.000
No, Redskin, go.

48:52.000 --> 48:56.000
Don't come nosing around here again, or you won't get off so easy.

48:56.000 --> 48:59.000
Creel has orders not to lead a posse anywhere.

49:02.000 --> 49:06.000
Say, Cory, do you think he was on the level about the sheriff being on Cavendish's trail?

49:06.000 --> 49:08.000
Of course he was on the level.

49:08.000 --> 49:10.000
Why do you think I signed to protect Cavendish?

49:10.000 --> 49:13.000
With all of our plans set for tonight, we can't let the sheriff mess things up.

49:13.000 --> 49:16.000
But if that Indian gets back to the sheriff and tells him that Creel...

49:16.000 --> 49:19.000
We're not going to get back to the sheriff, because you're going to see that he doesn't.

49:19.000 --> 49:20.000
Sergeant.

49:20.000 --> 49:21.000
Sammy.

49:21.000 --> 49:22.000
Oh, hi, Sammy.

49:22.000 --> 49:49.000
Nothing to worry about.

49:52.000 --> 50:12.000
Nothing like making sure, engine.

50:52.000 --> 51:02.000
It's Tonto, Silver. Tonto.

51:02.000 --> 51:08.000
Me follow trail, Kimo Chavi. Takes long time.

51:08.000 --> 51:09.000
Where's the posse?

51:09.000 --> 51:13.000
No posse. Sheriff's men in Colby wouldn't send men with me.

51:13.000 --> 51:15.000
Must be one of Cavendish's men.

51:15.000 --> 51:20.000
That's right. Him send men to ambush me. Let me take care of him.

51:20.000 --> 51:22.000
You find Cavendish?

51:22.000 --> 51:27.000
Yes. But his men rode down from the hills and surrounded the sheriff and Doc Drummond.

51:27.000 --> 51:28.000
We go back and round them up?

51:28.000 --> 51:33.000
No, there are too many of them. We'd need a whole troop of cavalry to capture them all.

51:33.000 --> 51:56.000
But I've got a plan, Tonto. Now, here's the idea. We'll circle back.

51:56.000 --> 52:01.000
That's Cavendish's horse. He must still be here. The rest of his men are out looking for me.

52:01.000 --> 52:03.000
Where Doc can share him?

52:03.000 --> 52:32.000
Over in the glen behind the brush. Come on, Tonto.

52:32.000 --> 52:48.000
That's Sherry's horse.

52:48.000 --> 52:51.000
Yes, Tonto. He still must be here with the Doc.

52:51.000 --> 52:55.000
I imagine that Cavendish is watching our friends until his men return.

52:55.000 --> 53:00.000
We'll sneak up to the glen. If Cavendish is there, I'll want to surprise him.

53:00.000 --> 53:03.000
Then you know what to do. Ahudau.

53:03.000 --> 53:32.000
We'll leave our horses here.

53:32.000 --> 53:41.000
Take it easy, you two. I may not be strong enough to travel, but I've got strength enough to pull a trigger.

53:41.000 --> 53:48.000
You won't get away with this. How long do you think honest people are going to condone activities like yours, Cavendish?

53:48.000 --> 53:56.000
You know I've got men in my office who have sworn never to rest until you're captured. Myself included, of course.

53:56.000 --> 54:08.000
And that masked man, he's one of my special secret deputies. Your men will never catch him.

54:08.000 --> 54:17.000
You know, Cavendish, the Doc here and I and the masked man, we know a lot more about you than you think we do.

54:17.000 --> 54:24.000
Why, there's handbills on you all over this territory, pasted on every rock and every tree. You'll never get out of here.

54:24.000 --> 54:31.000
And another thing. Woo-hoo!

54:31.000 --> 54:36.000
Atta boy, stranger. You're a sight for sore eyes. Yes. I thought we were goners.

54:36.000 --> 54:43.000
Who's the Indian? He's my friend. Are you both all right? Yeah, we're all right, yeah.

54:43.000 --> 54:47.000
Cavendish is our cold.

54:47.000 --> 54:51.000
Where you been, stranger? I was getting worried. I rode off on silver.

54:51.000 --> 54:55.000
Met Tonto coming from Colby. We sneaked in here the back way.

54:55.000 --> 55:00.000
Sure, if your man Creelan Town wouldn't send the posse. Him no believe, Tonto.

55:00.000 --> 55:08.000
Well, when that proves he's part of this murder and fiends band, then I can name more in town that are working with him.

55:08.000 --> 55:13.000
Well, anyway, we got Cavendish. We can load him in Doc's rig and take him on into town.

55:13.000 --> 55:17.000
Wait a minute, Sheriff. I want to capture more of the gang than just Cavendish.

55:17.000 --> 55:23.000
He's the leader. With help we can capture him and his entire band when they return.

55:23.000 --> 55:26.000
Take too long to get help from Colby. They'll all get away.

55:26.000 --> 55:33.000
There's help much closer than town, Sheriff. Tonto said he saw a cavalry detachment passing a short distance off.

55:33.000 --> 55:44.000
We'll get their help. I'll leave Cavendish here. He's out and too weak to travel anyway.

55:44.000 --> 55:51.000
Well, whatever you say. All right, let's go. Come on, Doc.

55:51.000 --> 55:54.000
Don't worry, you're not going to lose me.

56:04.000 --> 56:06.000
Which way to the cavalry, Mister?

56:06.000 --> 56:08.000
Take the right fork and circle back to Colby.

56:08.000 --> 56:11.000
To Colby? What about the cavalry?

56:11.000 --> 56:14.000
There are no cavalry. But you said they're passing through hereabouts.

56:14.000 --> 56:19.000
What I said was for Cavendish to hear. But he couldn't hear anything. He was unconscious.

56:19.000 --> 56:23.000
You should have examined him when I did, Doc. He was faking.

56:23.000 --> 56:27.000
Well, if there's no cavalry, just what in tarnation are you aiming to do?

56:27.000 --> 56:34.000
I'm trying to delay Cavendish's plan, Sheriff, to gain time. We have a lot to do tonight in Colby.

56:34.000 --> 56:41.000
All right, Doc. Let's get on with it. All right.

56:54.000 --> 56:58.000
Unless some of these men spotted the match man, Chief, at the sense he didn't head for Colby.

56:58.000 --> 57:01.000
I know he didn't. Say, what happened to the prisoners?

57:01.000 --> 57:06.000
The match man must have hidden a brush nearby. While you were out straining the eyes for him, he came back here with an engine friend.

57:06.000 --> 57:08.000
Trying to present loose, huh?

57:08.000 --> 57:12.000
Yeah. They all let out fast, but not for Colby. They went the opposite direction.

57:12.000 --> 57:13.000
Where did they go?

57:13.000 --> 57:17.000
I overheard the match man tell the sheriff there's a group of cavalry passing nearby.

57:17.000 --> 57:20.000
Now, they've gone off to get them to round us all up. So we got to work fast.

57:20.000 --> 57:24.000
Green, you, Sellers, and Shep, stick here with me. We'll go back to the hideout.

57:24.000 --> 57:28.000
The rest of you try to head off the prisoners. See if you can spot that cavalry, too.

57:28.000 --> 57:31.000
This is going to delay my plans concerning Colby a bit.

57:31.000 --> 57:34.000
I can't take any chances. Now get stirring.

57:45.000 --> 57:52.000
I sure hope Cleo didn't see us come in here. The sheriff's office is practically next door to mine, and I noticed the lights were still on.

57:52.000 --> 57:54.000
That's all for the good, Doc.

57:54.000 --> 57:59.000
It will give Tano a chance to sneak over there and see if anyone else is in the office with Cleo.

57:59.000 --> 58:04.000
While he is gone, we can make up that list of the names of the people we want to surprise.

58:04.000 --> 58:05.000
Me go now, Kimo Sabe.

58:05.000 --> 58:12.000
Be careful, Tano. Remember, Cleo is one of Cavendish's men. You know what happened the last time you tried to see him.

58:12.000 --> 58:16.000
Me be plenty careful. You make list ready. Tonto be back fast.

58:19.000 --> 58:23.000
Are we sure that we hid your horses and my wagon far enough out of town?

58:23.000 --> 58:25.000
If anybody gets wind right here, there'll be trouble.

58:25.000 --> 58:28.000
If we act fast enough, nobody will have time to stop us.

58:30.000 --> 58:36.000
All right, Sheriff. Write down the names of the new men in town. The ones who've been taking over all the top jobs.

58:37.000 --> 58:39.000
Well, first of all is Creel, of course.

58:40.000 --> 58:46.000
Put his name at the head of the list. If we take the jail first, we'll have a place to lock up all the others.

58:46.000 --> 58:50.000
And then there's Keeler. He took over the bank as soon as the judge was murdered.

58:50.000 --> 58:53.000
Keeler. Yes, old Jim told me about him.

58:53.000 --> 58:54.000
And what about him?

58:54.000 --> 58:57.000
He was put in charge of the telegraph all of a mighty sudden.

58:57.000 --> 58:59.000
Yes, I've heard of him too.

59:00.000 --> 59:02.000
Then there's Jessup. He's that new gunsmith.

59:03.000 --> 59:05.000
Peters, Duke, and Corey.

59:07.000 --> 59:09.000
Well, Tano, what did you find out?

59:09.000 --> 59:13.000
Me take Pete to window of Sheriff's office. See Creel in there with one man.

59:14.000 --> 59:15.000
Just two of them, eh?

59:15.000 --> 59:19.000
All right, Sheriff. Doc, we'll start with Creel and his friends.

59:19.000 --> 59:22.000
And if everything goes well, we'll end up with a whole Cavendish gang.

59:22.000 --> 59:23.000
All right, come on, boys.

59:30.000 --> 59:32.000
I wonder what's keeping Cavendish.

59:33.000 --> 59:35.000
Sure getting to be late enough.

59:35.000 --> 59:38.000
Oh, I don't know, Creel. Maybe him and the boys were held up.

59:38.000 --> 59:44.000
Probably just sent us a message or something.

59:45.000 --> 59:46.000
Who is it?

59:46.000 --> 59:48.000
It's me, Doc Trauma. An emergency.

59:48.000 --> 59:50.000
Talk it, let's use out of town, Pete.

59:50.000 --> 59:53.000
Quiet, you two. Keep your hands high.

59:53.000 --> 59:55.000
Say, what's going on here?

59:55.000 --> 59:58.000
Plenty, Creel. Tano, get Red's gun.

01:00:00.000 --> 01:00:03.000
We have reason to believe you two are working for a butch Cavendish.

01:00:03.000 --> 01:00:08.000
And I've got an idea those credentials you showed me to get your job will forge.

01:00:08.000 --> 01:00:10.000
Now wait a minute, you can't prove it.

01:00:10.000 --> 01:00:11.000
Shut up.

01:00:11.000 --> 01:00:13.000
Isn't that so? You're too red.

01:00:13.000 --> 01:00:15.000
Too red, I'm giving that to you. You made it, Creel's pal.

01:00:15.000 --> 01:00:16.000
That's it.

01:00:16.000 --> 01:00:17.000
All right.

01:00:22.000 --> 01:00:25.000
All right, Doc. We've got to hurry. It's getting light outside.

01:00:25.000 --> 01:00:42.000
Come on, Tano.

01:00:42.000 --> 01:00:44.000
Now, who's that?

01:00:44.000 --> 01:00:46.000
It's Doc Trauma.

01:00:47.000 --> 01:00:48.000
Yeah?

01:00:48.000 --> 01:00:50.000
Well, what are you doing here this time, Pete?

01:00:50.000 --> 01:00:52.000
Are you running the back now in place of Judge Knox?

01:00:52.000 --> 01:00:54.000
Yeah, but what's that got to do with it?

01:00:54.000 --> 01:00:56.000
Well, you're taking a gander the place is on fire.

01:00:56.000 --> 01:00:57.000
Fire?

01:00:57.000 --> 01:00:59.000
All right, Keeler, march.

01:00:59.000 --> 01:01:00.000
Huh?

01:01:06.000 --> 01:01:08.000
Keeler, check.

01:01:08.000 --> 01:01:11.000
One down and seven to go.

01:01:16.000 --> 01:01:20.000
Are you the gent that took over the telegraph office when Dick Anderson got here?

01:01:20.000 --> 01:01:21.000
What about it, Doc?

01:01:21.000 --> 01:01:23.000
Well, come on, quick. Your place is on fire.

01:01:23.000 --> 01:01:24.000
What?

01:01:24.000 --> 01:01:26.000
Put your hands up, Rivers.

01:01:30.000 --> 01:01:31.000
Fire?

01:01:31.000 --> 01:01:33.000
On your way, Joseph.

01:01:37.000 --> 01:01:39.000
No trace of the masked man and his friends, huh?

01:01:39.000 --> 01:01:41.000
No. We spent the whole night looking.

01:01:41.000 --> 01:01:42.000
What about the cavalry?

01:01:42.000 --> 01:01:45.000
Must have gone through here awful fast, Chief.

01:01:45.000 --> 01:01:47.000
Nary a sign of them now.

01:01:47.000 --> 01:01:50.000
Good. Then there's nothing to stop us from moving into Colby right now.

01:01:50.000 --> 01:01:51.000
Right.

01:01:51.000 --> 01:01:53.000
All right, let's go.

01:01:57.000 --> 01:02:00.000
All right, men, you heard the masked man.

01:02:00.000 --> 01:02:01.000
Everything goes as we figured.

01:02:01.000 --> 01:02:04.000
We'll have the Cavendish gang locked up by sundown.

01:02:04.000 --> 01:02:06.000
Hunter, are your men all set?

01:02:06.000 --> 01:02:08.000
Yes, Chief.

01:02:12.000 --> 01:02:14.000
Here they come.

01:02:14.000 --> 01:02:16.000
All right, boys, let's go get them.

01:02:16.000 --> 01:02:21.000
Run, killer.

01:02:30.000 --> 01:02:32.000
Move up.

01:02:35.000 --> 01:02:37.000
They're from Colby.

01:02:37.000 --> 01:02:38.000
And the masked man's leading them.

01:02:38.000 --> 01:02:40.000
We can take them without number.

01:02:40.000 --> 01:02:46.000
Let them have it, men.

01:03:10.000 --> 01:03:16.000
Let's go.

01:03:40.000 --> 01:03:46.000
Let's go.

01:04:10.000 --> 01:04:16.000
Let's go.

01:04:40.000 --> 01:04:46.000
Let's go.

01:05:10.000 --> 01:05:16.000
Let's go.

01:05:26.000 --> 01:05:28.000
You got me, stranger.

01:05:28.000 --> 01:05:32.000
Drop your gun, Cavendish.

01:05:32.000 --> 01:05:34.000
It's been a long chase.

01:05:34.000 --> 01:05:37.000
Now you're through for good.

01:05:37.000 --> 01:05:39.000
I guess you're right.

01:05:39.000 --> 01:05:40.000
Let's go.

01:05:40.000 --> 01:05:41.000
Where to?

01:05:41.000 --> 01:05:42.000
Back to the fight.

01:05:42.000 --> 01:05:44.000
Where'd you say?

01:05:44.000 --> 01:05:46.000
I said...

01:05:46.000 --> 01:05:50.000
Now get going.

01:05:54.000 --> 01:05:58.000
Put him in one.

01:05:58.000 --> 01:06:00.000
I sure will.

01:06:00.000 --> 01:06:04.000
Put him in two.

01:06:04.000 --> 01:06:05.000
Let's go.

01:06:05.000 --> 01:06:06.000
Name?

01:06:06.000 --> 01:06:07.000
Six Lambert.

01:06:07.000 --> 01:06:08.000
One.

01:06:08.000 --> 01:06:14.000
And I want to tell you that the whole town of Colby is proud of you two guns.

01:06:14.000 --> 01:06:20.000
Well, I appreciate that a lot, Jim, but we had very little income now, Sheriff.

01:06:20.000 --> 01:06:23.000
There he is, Sheriff.

01:06:23.000 --> 01:06:25.000
Hello, Cavendish.

01:06:25.000 --> 01:06:27.000
Didn't think you'd catch him, partner.

01:06:27.000 --> 01:06:30.000
Now we got the whole dang bunch.

01:06:30.000 --> 01:06:32.000
You and your men are to be congratulated, Sheriff.

01:06:32.000 --> 01:06:33.000
Thanks.

01:06:33.000 --> 01:06:36.000
But if it hadn't been for you, we'd have gotten nowhere.

01:06:36.000 --> 01:06:38.000
I'll be with you in just a minute.

01:06:38.000 --> 01:06:40.000
I'll take care of this one personally.

01:06:40.000 --> 01:06:42.000
Come on.

01:06:47.000 --> 01:06:49.000
It is over.

01:06:49.000 --> 01:06:51.000
Now, Cavendish can't catch a team of zombies.

01:06:51.000 --> 01:06:53.000
You take off, Matt.

01:06:53.000 --> 01:06:57.000
I'm going to continue to wear the mask and keep my identity a secret.

01:06:57.000 --> 01:06:58.000
For how long?

01:06:58.000 --> 01:07:00.000
Our job has just begun.

01:07:00.000 --> 01:07:03.000
We have a lot of trails to follow.

01:07:03.000 --> 01:07:05.000
That's good.

01:07:05.000 --> 01:07:10.000
Sheriff, before the lockup, do you mind telling me who the masked man is?

01:07:10.000 --> 01:07:12.000
Wouldn't mind at all, except that...

01:07:12.000 --> 01:07:16.000
Hey, where'd the masked man go?

01:07:16.000 --> 01:07:19.000
He and his Indian pal are going out to get the horses, Sheriff.

01:07:19.000 --> 01:07:22.000
Well, I guess he isn't one to stick around for a party.

01:07:22.000 --> 01:07:24.000
But who is he?

01:07:24.000 --> 01:07:35.000
I don't rightly know his real name, but I've heard him called the Lone Ranger.

01:07:35.000 --> 01:07:37.000
Get him in the car.

01:07:37.000 --> 01:07:59.160
Wireless camera are audio and video
